codex
The patch adds broad variadic and ParamSpec support, but several valid supported forms still produce incorrect runtime resolutions. These are correctness issues in the new feature surface, so the patch is not fully correct.

Full review comments:

- [P2] Expand fixed tuple unpacks when binding `*Ts` — /home/g/wt/paramsight/active/merge_reviewed_and_variadic/src/paramsight/_paramsight.py:321-325
  When a variadic specialization uses an unpacked fixed tuple (`V[*tuple[int, str]]` or `V[Unpack[tuple[int, str]]]`), the absorbed slice is treated as a single argument or as unresolved instead of being expanded. For `class V[*Ts](B[tuple[*Ts]])`, this makes `V[*tuple[int, str]].x_type` resolve to `tuple[*tuple[int, str]]` (and the `Unpack[...]` spelling to `NoDefault`) rather than `tuple[int, str]`, so concrete, finite TypeVarTuple bindings are silently mis-resolved.

- [P2] Coerce ParamSpec list arguments before binding — /home/g/wt/paramsight/active/merge_reviewed_and_variadic/src/paramsight/_paramsight.py:178-180
  For the explicit ParamSpec list spelling, Python passes the list members here in source form; this helper only substitutes them. A user writing `C[[None, "Foo"]]` therefore gets `Callable[[None, 'Foo'], R]`, while the shorthand `C[None, "Foo"]` resolves to `Callable[[NoneType, ForwardRef('Foo')], R]`; the two spellings should agree just like scalar type parameter bindings.

- [P2] Normalize ParamSpec defaults as parameter lists — /home/g/wt/paramsight/active/merge_reviewed_and_variadic/src/paramsight/_paramsight.py:276-284
  Only TypeVarTuple defaults get special handling here, so a ParamSpec default such as `**P = [T]` falls through to the scalar TypeVar default path. Because `_substitute_typevars` does not walk plain lists, `class C[T = int, **P = [T]](B[Callable[P, R]])` leaves `P` as `[T]` and the descriptor reports unresolved instead of `Callable[[int], R]`.

- [P2] Preserve Concatenate prefixes when `P` is ellipsis — /home/g/wt/paramsight/active/merge_reviewed_and_variadic/src/paramsight/_paramsight.py:196-197
  When the tail ParamSpec in `Concatenate[prefix, P]` is bound to `...`, returning a bare `Ellipsis` makes the Callable branch rebuild `Callable[..., R]` and drops the already-resolved prefix. For `class C[**P](B[Callable[Concatenate[int, P], R]])`, `C[...]` should retain the required leading `int` as `Callable[Concatenate[int, ...], R]`; otherwise callers see a less precise signature.
The patch adds broad variadic and ParamSpec support, but several valid supported forms still produce incorrect runtime resolutions. These are correctness issues in the new feature surface, so the patch is not fully correct.

